21:20:06 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes—this is experimentally real, not just theoretical. Artificial-light rainbows have been observed and photographed, especially from **car headlights, spotlights, and bright flashlights shining into spray or mist**. They are sometimes called *night rainbows* or *artificial rainbows*. The reason you rarely see one from an ordinary streetlight is mostly geometry and visibility: - A rainbow is seen only from droplets that send light back toward **your particular eye** at about the rainbow angle. The light source needs to be roughly **behind or near your head**, with mist/rain in front of you. - A streetlamp is usually overhead or off to one side, not aligned with your viewing direction. - Rain is spread over a large depth range. Because a nearby lamp’s rays diverge strongly, the rainbow angle varies with distance, making the colored arc smear out. - Many streetlights are not truly white. Old sodium lamps are nearly monochromatic yellow, so there is little or no color spectrum to separate. - Even white streetlights are often not intense enough in the relevant direction compared with background glare. You can sometimes make one yourself under good conditions: 1. Go somewhere dark. 2. Use a bright white flashlight, preferably a focused LED beam. 3. Hold it very close to your eyes or mount it near the camera/eye line. 4. Shine it forward into a fine mist from a hose, sprinkler, fountain, or waterfall several metres away. 5. Look into the illuminated mist. With a sufficiently fine, dense spray, you may see a faint colored arc or part of a circle centered around the direction the flashlight is pointing. A camera, particularly with a longer exposure, may reveal it more easily than your eyes. A normal flashlight aimed casually at nearby rain usually will not show a clear bow, so your experience is entirely expected—the effect requires unusually favorable alignment, bright light, dark surroundings, and suitable droplets.
