21:20:34 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=0 miss=1265
21:20:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Because textbooks usually teach the **standard natural case**, not every possible version of the phenomenon. “Sunlight + rain” is the most useful description because it is overwhelmingly the situation in which people actually encounter a clear rainbow: - The Sun is bright enough to make the bow visible. - Sunlight has a broad range of wavelengths, producing the familiar spectrum. - The Sun is effectively infinitely far away, so its rays are nearly parallel. That makes the standard 40–42° rainbow geometry simple to explain. - Rain provides a huge cloud of droplets over a large area, so the arc can be large and obvious. Using “the Sun” also lets textbooks state the familiar rule: *the Sun is behind the observer and rain is ahead.* Strictly, the underlying rule is: *a bright, sufficiently broad-spectrum source is behind the observer and droplets are ahead.* But that is more abstract and less useful for an introductory explanation. They also often leave out other real cases for the same reason they omit many variants: - **Moonbows**: rainbows made by moonlight. - **Fogbows**: broad, pale bows from tiny fog droplets. - **Glories**: colored rings around an observer’s shadow on clouds. - **Double and supernumerary rainbows**. - **Artificial-source bows** from lamps, vehicle lights, or controlled spray. That said, “any light source makes a normal rainbow” would be an oversimplification too. The source’s size, spectrum, brightness, distance, and placement strongly affect whether an identifiable bow can form. The Sun happens to satisfy all those conditions extraordinarily well, which is why it dominates both nature and textbook explanations.
