21:20:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That intuition is basically right in an everyday sense: **the rainbows people normally mean are sunlight rainbows**, and artificial-light versions are rare, usually weak, and often not recognizably rainbow-shaped. The important nuance is that this is not because sunlight has a unique rainbow-making property. It is because the Sun is uniquely good at supplying the conditions needed on a large scale: - It is extremely bright. - It is effectively very far away, so it illuminates all the rain with nearly parallel rays. - It is large enough and white enough to give a stable, broad colored arc. - It lights a vast volume of rain at once. - It is common to have rain in one part of the sky and low Sun in another. A streetlamp may illuminate only a small patch of rain, from a nearby and awkward angle, often with imperfect color. Even if the same droplet physics occurs, there may be no big, stable arc for your eye to recognize. So if “real rainbow” means the familiar, outdoor arch across the sky, then yes: for practical purposes, those are almost always made by the Sun—occasionally by the Moon. Artificial sources are genuine instances of the same optics, but they are not a common replacement for the ordinary experience people call “seeing a rainbow.”
